y have a Chow containing T2, so the extra T2 is redundant for sequences. Unshown T2: 2 left, so discarding it might be safe, but T2 is a simple tile that could fit into opponents\u2019 Chows.  \n\n4. **Play B2 or B3** \u2013 You have two of each; keeping them could lead to a pung or pair for B2/B3. Unshown B2: 4 left, B3: 4 left, so good chances to improve. Discarding one would break a potential pung and reduce hand flexibility.  \n\n5. **Play F4** \u2013 North wind; unshown F4: 2 left, not a prevailing/seat wind, so less valuable. Discarding F4 is safe.  \n\nThe priority should be to maintain hand flexibility while discarding safer tiles. Among safe discards, F1 and J3 are honors that are less likely to help opponents form Chows. But F1 has extra point potential if kept, so maybe keep it for now. B2/B3 are more useful for building a hand (can form pungs or pairs).  \n\n**Reason:**  \nRight now your hand is fragmented: you need two more melds and a pair. Keeping duplicate tiles (B2, B3) gives more chances to draw a third for a pung or use them in a pair. Honors F1 and F4 are singletons; discarding one reduces clutter without harming sequence potential.  \nSince F1 is double wind, it\u2019s worth keeping slightly longer\u2014if you draw another F1, you get a valuable pung. But if you discard it now, it\u2019s still safe.  \nHowever, looking at tile availability: W3, B4, B6, B7, etc. are dead, so suits are getting sparse. A Half Flush (only honor + one suit) seems difficult because your hand has mixed suits. \u201cAll Types\u201d (three suits + winds + dragons) is possible with your current mix.  \nFor speed, discard a less useful honor first. F4 is the least valuable honor here (not prevailing/seat, not dragon), and unshown F4 count is low, so opponents are unlikely to be waiting for it.  \n\n**Answer:** Play F4"}
